    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve for \\(ab + ac + bc\\) given that \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\) are the roots of the polynomial equation \\(3x^3 - 3x^2 + 11x - 8 = 0\\), we can use Vieta's formulas. Vieta's formulas relate the coefficients of a polynomial to sums and products of its roots. For a cubic polynomial of the form \\(ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d = 0\\), the roots \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\) satisfy:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\na + b + c &= -\\frac{b}{a}, \\\\\nab + ac + bc &= \\frac{c}{a}, \\\\\nabc &= -\\frac{d}{a}.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nGiven the polynomial \\(3x^3 - 3x^2 + 11x - 8 = 0\\), we can identify the coefficients as \\(a = 3\\), \\(b = -3\\), \\(c = 11\\), and \\(d = -8\\).\n\nWe need to find \\(ab + ac + bc\\):\n\n1. Identify \\(ab\\), \\(ac\\), and \\(bc\\) from Vieta's formulas:\n   \\[\n   ab = \\frac{c}{a}, \\quad ac = \\frac{c}{a}, \\quad bc = \\frac{c}{a}.\n   \\]\n\n2. Sum these values:\n   \\[\n   ab + ac + bc = \\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{c}{a} = \\frac{3c}{a}.\n   \\]\n\n3. Substitute the known values:\n   \\[\n   ab + ac + bc = \\frac{3c}{a} = \\frac{3 \\times 11}{3} = 11.\n   \\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{11}\n\\]",
